==English==
{{wikipedia|Vocative case}} {{wikipedia|Vocative expression}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*wekʷ-}}
From Late {{inh|en|enm}}, borrowed from {{der|en|frm|vocatif}}, from {{der|en|la|vocātīvus||for calling}}; a calque of Ancient Greek {{m|grc|κλητῐκή||for calling; vocative case}} &ndash; from {{m|la|voco|vocāre|to call}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro||*wokʷ-}}, [[o-grade]] of {{m|ine-pro|*wekʷ-|t=give vocal utterance, speak}}. See Latin {{m|la|vōx}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|väk'ətĭv|vōk'ətĭv|a=GenAm}}, {{IPA|en|/ˈvɑkətɪv/|/ˈvoʊkətɪv/}}<!--common but sometimes proscribed, see November 2022 TR-->
* {{enPR|vŏk'ətĭv|a=RP}}, {{IPA|en|/ˈvɒkətɪv/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-vocative.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈvakətɪv/|a=Dublin}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Of or pertaining to calling; used in calling or [[vocation]].
# {{lb|en|grammar}} Used in address; [[appellative]]; said of the case or form of the noun, pronoun, or adjective by which a person or thing is addressed. In English, it is indicated by an addressee–address separation comma, or by the particle {{m|en|O|id=vocative}} before the addressee. As examples: "sir" in "What is the matter, sir?", "Mother" in "Mother, listen!", and "O Lord". Compare [[et tu, et]].
]]

